From 49a5c1af23f431aba75ed01d2be6570d2e961d98 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: iam <iam@iashur.internal>
Date: Sat, 8 Aug 2026 22:36:45 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] 360 viewer: digital zoom (controller thumbstick Y, keyboard
 up/down)

Zoom on panoramic/flat content, mapped to the vertical thumbstick axis that
was completely unused (QueueSeek already owns X on both oculus/touch_controller
and microsoft/motion_controller) - same hysteresis-latch shape as seek/pause
(push past 0.7, must fall back under 0.3 to fire again), stepping the zoom
multiplicatively by 1.15x per crossing, clamped to [0.5, 4.0]. Keyboard: up/down
arrows (same ESC '[' A/B disambiguation main.cpp already does for C/D), with
'^'/'v' as a plain-key fallback mirroring '<'/'>' for frame step, and '0' to
reset to 1x (pairs with '1' for normal speed).

Implementation: PlayerControl::Zoom() stores the multiplier as a bit-pattern
atomic (RecenterYaw's trick), with 0 as an "unset -> 1.0" sentinel like
g_lastInteractionMs already uses. Threaded to the shader via panoFov.z, which
was unused padding (see vulkan_utils.h) - no push-constant growth, same
reasoning as mode.y/mode.z for the progress bar. frag.glsl divides the
projection-mapping coordinate (az/el for 360/180, screen xy for flat) by zoom
before the existing mapping formulas: the sphere/screen never changes, just
how much of it one physical ray picks up.

Tested by the agent: keyboard zoom in x3 / out x1 / reset, via a fake pty
against the live stack (real monado-service, real G2 hardware) - log shows
the exact expected sequence (1.15 -> 1.32 -> 1.52 -> 1.32 -> 1.00x) and a
clean quit. The action registers correctly against real hardware too: "Zoom
action is bound to 'Left/Right Oculus Touch Controller Thumbstick'" in the
log, no collision with Seek on the same physical stick. Ruled out one false
lead: a vkDestroySemaphore validation warning on quit is pre-existing on the
unmodified baseline (checked by stashing this commit and reproducing it with
zero zoom keys pressed) - not introduced here.

NOT yet verified: the controller thumbstick Y sign (assumed +y = up = zoom
in, standard OpenXR/gamepad convention, but never confirmed live - flip the
ZoomIn()/ZoomOut() branches in PollActions if it's backwards) and the actual
visual result in the headset (does zooming in look like a clean magnification,
does zooming out look acceptably fisheye-y rather than broken). Needs a human
with the headset on, per this project's core rule.
